WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.893
Lesson one is tool design.

2
00:00:02.223 --> 00:00:05.245
It's the contract between a model and the system it drives.

3
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:10.560
Picture a plant shop whose support agent has two tools, both described as search.

4
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.235
A customer asks where her fern is, and the agent searches the product catalogue.

5
00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:17.585
The team wants a bigger model.

6
00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:21.065
But a vague tool is far more often the cause than a weak model.

7
00:00:21.865 --> 00:00:23.820
The model never runs anything itself.

8
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.327
It emits a request: a tool name, plus JSON arguments matching a declared schema.

9
00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:33.160
Your runtime validates, authorises, and runs it,

10
00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.858
then returns the result as an observation.

11
00:00:36.188 --> 00:00:39.295
That split underpins every security control in an agent.

12
00:00:40.095 --> 00:00:44.845
A strict JSON Schema lets code reject a malformed call before it runs.

13
00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:48.197
The error goes back to the model, which can correct itself.

14
00:00:48.997 --> 00:00:52.063
The schema also lets providers constrain decoding,

15
00:00:52.113 --> 00:00:54.494
so arguments are valid by construction.

16
00:00:54.824 --> 00:00:59.041
And enums, limits, and formats describe valid values better than prose.

17
00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:03.332
The description is a prompt, read just as the model picks a tool.

18
00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:07.089
Say what it does, when to use it, when not to, and what it returns.

19
00:01:07.889 --> 00:01:12.213
So the shop adds negative scope: order lookup is for a customer's own orders.

20
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:15.522
Do not use it for products; use product search instead.

21
00:01:16.322 --> 00:01:18.519
Too many similar tools blur together,

22
00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:23.376
yet one giant tool with a mode switch just moves the confusion into an argument.

23
00:01:23.706 --> 00:01:26.130
So climb a ladder: sharper descriptions,

24
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.160
merge or split, group, retrieve, then sub-agents.

25
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.326
Then a refund call times out after the refund went through, and the agent retries.

26
00:01:34.656 --> 00:01:38.082
An idempotency key, which the server deduplicates on,

27
00:01:38.132 --> 00:01:40.771
means the customer is refunded only once.

28
00:01:41.571 --> 00:01:45.895
Separate read and write tools, and put destructive ones behind human approval.

29
00:01:46.225 --> 00:01:51.351
And make errors actionable: "expected YYYY-MM-DD" lets the model fix itself,

30
00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:53.599
while "400 Bad Request" does not.

31
00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:57.890
Authorisation belongs in the tool, never in the system prompt.

32
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.630
Check the user's permissions on every call,

33
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.375
the only defence that survives prompt injection.

34
00:02:04.175 --> 00:02:07.764
Exam trap: strict typing does not eliminate hallucination,

35
00:02:07.814 --> 00:02:11.528
because it guarantees a well-formed call, not the right one.

36
00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:14.048
Read on for the full tool-design checklist.
